\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The dressed-star family $M_{\rm  ADM}(\omega )$ at the ultraweak rung. At equal frequency the phantom star is always the heavier in magnitude ($20\%$ at $\omega =0.55$, $4\%$ at $\omega =0.80$)---repulsive self-gravity puffs it out, a gravitational effect that vanishes in the weak-field limit. The branch ends near $\omega \approx 0.52$--$0.53$ (shaded); no dressed star exists below it at this rung. Stars ($\star $): the equal-$|M_{\rm  ADM}|$ pair actually evolved, canonical at $\omega =0.550$ ($M=+0.063951$) and phantom at $\omega =0.56598$ ($M=-0.063950$), matched to one part in $10^5$.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:family}{{1}{3}{The dressed-star family $M_{\rm ADM}(\omega )$ at the ultraweak rung. At equal frequency the phantom star is always the heavier in magnitude ($20\%$ at $\omega =0.55$, $4\%$ at $\omega =0.80$)---repulsive self-gravity puffs it out, a gravitational effect that vanishes in the weak-field limit. The branch ends near $\omega \approx 0.52$--$0.53$ (shaded); no dressed star exists below it at this rung. Stars ($\star $): the equal-$|M_{\rm ADM}|$ pair actually evolved, canonical at $\omega =0.550$ ($M=+0.063951$) and phantom at $\omega =0.56598$ ($M=-0.063950$), matched to one part in $10^5$}{figure.1}{}}
